const t="maharashtra-rajya-natya-spardha",h=2026,e="Read in full (Marathi, translated to plain English, original terms in brackets the first time) the portal home page, the Directorate competitions page, the 2026-27 State Drama Competition rules (7 pages including the 10 August 2026 entries notice), the 2026-27 State Balnatya and Divyang Balnatya rules (4 pages), the Marathi One-Act Play (Ekankika) rules (8 pages, Government Resolution of 29 September 2025 with prize statements 1 and 2) and the 9 January 2017 Government Resolution with prize annexes 2 to 10. The PDFs have no usable text layer, so every page was read from rendered images. Edition facts are Variables: entry site, closing date, start dates, deposits, travel and production costs, Directorate and divisional office contacts and prizes. Prize amounts for the drama and Balnatya competitions are those of the 2017 Government Resolution, the only source that prints them; the 2026-27 rule books print no prize amounts, so the 2017 figures are marked as past figures to confirm. Prize amounts for the one-act play are from the 2025 resolution. The 15 November and 16 November start dates differ (Directorate page and the dated 2026 notice): the newer 16 November 2026 is used and 15 November is in the Dates item. The competition page says the entry window is 1 to 31 August; the 2026 notice says 31 August 2026. Not copied: names of the Minister, Secretary and Director in the notice, committee and examiner names, results lists with winners (2022 press results), the pension and scholarship schemes printed on the same web page that are not part of the competition, and the sample counts of registered institutions. The portal's counts of entries received (542 Marathi, 27 Music, 93 Hindi, 28 Sanskrit, 601 Balnatya, 16 Divyang Balnatya, 97 one-act) are not rules and are not captured. The rule books print no marking criteria, no marks per category and no time table of centres. The template's Balnatya group ages 8 to 16 differ from the printed under-15 rule. Rule books were not read for the professional competition; only the portal and the Directorate page describe it. The affidavit (undertaking) forms listed on the portal were not read. The 2017 resolution's daily allowances for examiners (Rs 400 a day) and artists (Rs 150 a day) are old figures replaced in the 2026-27 rules (Rs 300 a day for stay), so they are not used.",a=JSON.parse(`{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"Maharashtra State Drama Competition (Rajya Natya Spardha) 2026-27, run by the Directorate of Cultural Affairs. Read these rules before you register your institution.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"Entries are made online at {{EntrySite}} by drama institutions, schools and colleges, with all required documents, from 1 August until {{EntriesClose}}. After the closing date no entry is accepted in any case. An entry cannot be filed without the required documents or with incomplete details. The 2026-27 competitions start on {{CompetitionStart}}. The rules bind every institution. An institution that breaks them is declared ineligible.","Notes":["The 2026-27 edition: 65th amateur (hausi) Marathi, Marathi Music (Sangeet), Hindi and Sanskrit drama competitions, 23rd State Balnatya (children's drama) competition and 8th State Divyang Balnatya (children with disabilities) competition.","If an institution that has been selected does not perform at the centre and on the date fixed by the Government, the deposit paid with its entry is paid into the Government treasury.","Questions: {{DirectorateContact}}."],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"General Rules","Items":["The competition is run by the Directorate of Cultural Affairs, Government of Maharashtra. It began in 1954-55. Its aim is to give young artists a proper platform and to spread drama art at every level.","The Directorate holds the competition at 19 centres in the State and 1 centre in Goa, 20 centres in all. It also holds the Hindi, Music, Sanskrit, Balnatya and Divyang Balnatya competitions.","The Directorate holds seven kinds of drama competition: amateur Marathi, amateur Hindi, amateur Music (Sangeet), amateur Sanskrit, amateur Balnatya, amateur Divyang Balnatya and professional Marathi.","The Government has the final right to accept or refuse a play proposed for the competition. If the Government refuses a play, it does not have to give the reasons.","The decision of the Government about the prizes of the competition is final.","The Government can cancel any entry without giving a reason.","If the Government asks, through the Theatre Performance Examination Board (Rangabhoomi Prayog Parinirikshan Mandal), that some parts or sentences of the play be removed, they must be removed.","A play that was shown at an earlier Natya Mahotsav and won a prize cannot be shown again by the same institution and the same artists.","An institution that does not follow the rules and conditions it gave in its entry is declared ineligible for the next drama competitions.","The duration of a Marathi or Hindi play shown at the festival is not less than 2 hours and not more than 3 hours, including intervals. Each interval is 15 minutes. A play can have no more than two intervals.","A Sanskrit play is not less than 45 minutes and not more than one hour. It has no interval.","A Music Marathi play is more than 3 hours and less than 4 hours.","A Balnatya play is not less than 25 minutes and not more than 45 minutes.","A play that the institution has already shown more than ten times before the competition is not accepted for the State drama competition.","A play that has a woman's role played by a man, or a man's role played by a woman, is not accepted unless the script says so. For Sanskrit competitions and school institutions only, this rule is relaxed.","An institution can enter only one language competition. No change is made in the list of artists after acceptance without the prior approval of the Director. The cast that performed in the preliminary round must perform in the final."]},{"Key":"eligibility","Title":"Eligibility","Items":["Amateur drama: the institution must have existed for at least two years.","Amateur drama: the institution must be registered under the Maharashtra Societies Registration Act, 1960 (Maharashtra co-op Society Registration Act as printed) or the Bombay Public Trusts Act, 1950, and must give the date and number of registration.","Amateur drama: or the institution must be the drama department of a college attached to a university in Maharashtra.","Amateur drama: or it must be a Sanskrit school (pathshala) or a secondary school. This applies to the Sanskrit drama competition only.","Amateur drama: or it must be a recognised association of employees of a government or semi-government department, or the drama or cultural department of such an association.","Primary and secondary schools are not admitted to any competition other than the Sanskrit drama competition.","The institution must not be a professional drama company. An institution run by a private owner or by more than one person in partnership, mainly for financial profit, counts as a professional drama company.","The aim of the institution's constitution must include the growth of cultural work, or the institution must be a drama or cultural department set up under the constitution of the institution. This does not apply to the employees' association, college and school categories above.","The institution must have shown at least two plays in the two years before the last date for accepting entries. Two or more one-act plays shown at the same time count as one play.","An institution that is registered outside Maharashtra can take part only in the Marathi drama competition, if it follows the rules and conditions. For other cases, the Director of Cultural Affairs decides.","For a competition in any language to be held, at least five valid entries must be received.","All artists in a play proposed for the competition must be amateur and must be authorised members of the institution.","All artists in the competition must be local residents of the district concerned. An Aadhaar card or a Government-accepted proof of residence must be attached to the application. An artist who is not a permanent resident of the district but lives there for work or study is accepted as a local artist with valid proof of the work or study attached.","Amateur artist: an artist who does not depend on acting in drama or cinema as his or her main means of living. An artist who, before the competition, worked for more than three months in a drama or cinema company on a monthly salary or contract, or took part in more than 90 performances on a night wage, is not an amateur. An artist who worked as a professional actor in a drama or cinema company and has retired is not an amateur, and the period after retirement is not counted.","An institution that is an employees' association of a department cannot use persons outside the association in its play, except members of its own drama or cultural department. Outside members who are made regular members as that department's rules allow can act in the play.","Sanskrit competition: if the institution is a school or a pathshala, teachers and former students cannot take part in the play. If they do, the institution is treated as a non-school institution and is ineligible for the prizes.","An artist cannot act in more than one play in the same language in the competition. The Marathi Music drama competition is counted as a separate competition for this rule.","An artist or technician who wins first prizes three or more times in a row in one department at the final of the Marathi, Sanskrit, Music or Hindi competition is counted as an honoured winner (Sanman Vijeta) and is not eligible for the next prize in that department. Writers are not included.","Balnatya: at least five valid entries must be received for the competition to be held.","Balnatya: the institution, school or college must be registered and must have existed for at least two years. The Director of Cultural Affairs can relax this period if the work of the institution is creative and supports children's theatre.","Balnatya: primary schools and secondary schools can apply. The applicant should if possible have got at least one prize at district level.","Balnatya: for the Balnatya competition, the child artists are under 15 years of age. For the Divyang Balnatya competition, children with disabilities who are studying up to Class 10 are eligible. The age condition for technicians is relaxed.","Balnatya: an institution, school or college can enter one Balnatya competition only. A child artist in a play of the competition cannot act in another Balnatya of the same State competition.","Balnatya: a play that has won a prize at an earlier Balnatya competition cannot be shown again by the same institution or school, but other institutions or schools can show it.","Balnatya: a child artist or technician who wins first prizes three or more times in a row in one department at the final is counted as an honoured winner and is not eligible for the next prize in that department. Writers are not included.","One-act play (Ekankika) for college students: colleges in Maharashtra can take part in the Marathi one-act play competition.","One-act play: only students who are studying in a college can take part. A proof that the artist is a student of the college must be attached. Former students of the college cannot take part.","One-act play: a college can send entries for more than one one-act play. The students in a one-act play must be students of the college that sent the entry. Professors and persons from the college office cannot take part.","One-act play: a one-act play must have at least two characters.","One-act play: a one-act play that has been shown more than ten times elsewhere, as a one-act play or as a full-length play, before the competition is not accepted.","One-act play: a college that has shown a one-act play that won a prize at an earlier Directorate one-act competition cannot show the same one-act play again (in a new group). Artists and technicians of that earlier performance cannot show the same play through another college. This rule starts from the second year of the competition.","One-act play: no artist of a college can act in more than one one-act play in the competition.","One-act play: for the competition to be held, at least 40 valid entries must be received."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"How to Register","Items":["Entries are filed online at {{EntrySite}}, in the prescribed form, with the required documents, within the period fixed. Entries not filed within the period are not considered.","The entry window is from 1 August to {{EntriesClose}} each year. Entry forms and rules are online on the website. Registered amateur drama institutions and institutions that took part in the competition the year before can file entries.","After the closing date no entry is accepted in any case. An online entry cannot be filed if the required documents are not attached or if the details are not filled in fully.","Amateur drama entry: attach one readable copy of the book of the play. An entry with an unreadable copy is not accepted.","Amateur drama entry: attach the certificate of the Theatre Performance Examination Board for the play. An entry without it is not accepted.","Amateur drama entry: attach the full and correct names of all artists in the play, with the roles they play, and the names of the director and of the persons responsible for set, sound and lighting. Changes made later must be sent as revised names three weeks before the competition starts.","Amateur drama entry: say whether the play is original, translated or adapted from another play, and name the author and the language of the original play. If it is based on a novel, story or other literary form, say so. Attach the author's letter of permission. An entry without it is not accepted.","Amateur drama entry: if the play is new and written especially for the festival, say so clearly.","Amateur drama entry: the proposed play must be named, with the name of its author. If the play is translated, give the name of the original author. If the Government does not accept the play named in the entry, the institution must propose another play.","Balnatya entry: file the application in the prescribed form. An institution, school or college can enter one Balnatya competition only.","Balnatya entry: attach one readable copy of the script of the play. The play does not need to have been shown before. The first performance can also be at the competition.","Balnatya entry: attach the certificate of the Theatre Performance Examination Board for the play. An entry without it is not accepted.","Balnatya entry: attach the full and correct names of all artists with their age and the roles they play, and the names of the persons responsible for direction, set, sound and lighting, background music, costume, writing and make-up. Changes made later must be sent three weeks before the competition starts.","Balnatya entry: say whether the play is original, translated or adapted, name the author and the original author if it is adapted, and attach the permission letter of the author (and of the original author for an adaptation). If the play is new and written especially for the Balnatya festival, say so clearly.","One-act play entry: file the entry online. Attach one readable copy of the script and the certificate of the Theatre Performance Examination Board. Without this certificate the one-act play cannot be shown at the preliminary round.","One-act play entry: attach the list of all artists and technicians with their roles and mobile numbers. Any change must be sent seven days before the performance.","One-act play entry: say whether the play is original, translated or adapted, and whether it is based on a novel or story. If the script is new and has never been performed in any theatre before, say so clearly. Such a script is then eligible for the one-act play writing prize.","One-act play entry: all students taking part must carry the college identity card during the competition. If asked, the group leader must show the identity cards of all students.","Once the schedule of performances is announced, no college can cancel its performance.","After the institution is selected for the competition, no change is made in the list of artists without the prior approval of the Director of Cultural Affairs.","A selected institution must perform its play on the day, at the place and at the time given, and must promise not to cancel it for any reason."]},{"Key":"fees","Title":"Deposits and Costs","Items":["Amateur drama: a deposit of {{DepositAmateur}} is paid online (UPI) with the entry. It is returned to institutions that perform the full play. It is paid into the Government treasury for institutions that cancel the performance at the last moment.","Balnatya and Divyang Balnatya: a deposit of {{DepositBalnatya}} is paid online (UPI) with the entry. It is returned to institutions, schools and colleges that perform the full Balnatya. It is paid into the Government treasury for those that cancel at the last moment.","One-act play: a deposit of {{DepositEkankika}} is paid online with the entry. It is returned to colleges that become ineligible, including in the training round, and to colleges that perform the full one-act play in the preliminary round. A college that cancels its performance after the schedule is announced, except in an exceptional situation with a strong reason, loses the deposit to the Government treasury and cannot take part in the next year's one-act competition.","No entry fee is printed in the rules, only the deposit. Money paid to institutions is under Money Paid to Competitors."]},{"Key":"payments","Title":"Money Paid to Competitors","Items":["Amateur drama: an institution that performs a full play in the preliminary round gets {{ProductionCostPrelim}} as production cost for sets, sound and light, transport of materials, labour and attractive sets.","Amateur drama: a local institution selected for the festival does not get travel cost or daily allowance. An institution that is told to perform in another town gets the cost of travel to and fro and of food and lodging. Travel cost is paid at second class rail fare or ordinary State Transport fare. Food and lodging is paid at {{BoardingAllowance}} for each day. The number of days is told later.","Amateur drama: travel cost is paid for the number of artists who take part in the play and act in it, limited to 20 artists (10 for Sanskrit drama), and in addition eight others such as the director, prompter and organiser. For a music play in which the characters sing directly on stage, the rule says the cost is given to twelve persons. The Director can change these limits if more artists are needed.","Amateur drama: the cost is paid after the institution gives true copies of the rail or State Transport tickets it bought. If the institution travels by rail at a concession rate, only the concession cost is paid.","Amateur drama: an institution that travels 250 kilometres or more also gets the cost of sleeper berth reservation.","Balnatya: a local institution, school or college does not get travel cost or daily allowance. One told to perform in another town gets travel to and fro within a limit of 28 persons (organiser, director, prompter, artists, technicians and others), with true copies of the tickets. At most 20 artists and 8 technicians get the actual rail or State Transport fare and food and lodging at {{BoardingAllowance}} a day.","Balnatya: an institution that performs a full Balnatya in the preliminary round gets {{ProductionCostPrelim}} for sets, sound and light, transport of materials, labour and attractive sets.","One-act play: no travel cost or production cost is paid for the training round. After the training round, a college that performs the full one-act play in the preliminary round gets {{EkankikaProductionCost}} for sound, light, transport, labour and sets.","One-act play: a college chosen for the preliminary or final round that is told to perform away from its registered place gets travel to and fro and a daily allowance for food and lodging of {{EkankikaAllowance}} for each person. Travel is paid at second class rail fare or ordinary State Transport fare. It is limited to 15 artists and 10 technicians.","One-act play: the Directorate gives a sound system, 8 spot lights and 4 levels free of cost for the one-act plays. Any other equipment must be arranged by the college.","One-act play: winning colleges must show one or more plays on the invitation of the Directorate after the competition. They are paid an honorarium for the show, travel cost and daily allowance.","Marathi and Music winners: the winning institutions of the first, second and third prizes in the final Marathi and Marathi Music competitions must show the play at up to five other centres, apart from the regional and final centres. For this, a grant of {{WinnerShowGrant}} is paid, for plays that have a new script only. The institution is fully responsible for the performance and for the advertisement, which must say that the play is a winner of the Maharashtra State Drama Festival. Proof of performances must be given to the Government. The shows must be held within six months after the prize ceremony, apart from the competition centres. Tickets at Rs 20 and Rs 15 can be sold to cover the costs. The travel cost and daily allowance rule applies.","Winning institutions of the first prize at the final (Music, Marathi, Hindi, Sanskrit) must perform one or more shows if the Government asks, after the competition."]},{"Key":"dates","Title":"Dates and Program","Items":["Entries: 1 August to {{EntriesClose}} each year.","Amateur Marathi, Hindi, Music and Sanskrit drama competitions start on {{CompetitionStart}}. The Directorate's competition page says the competition starts from 15 November each year.","23rd Balnatya competition, preliminary round: from {{BalnatyaPrelimStart}} at 10 centres.","65th amateur Hindi, Music and Sanskrit drama competitions, final round: one centre each, from {{FinalRoundStart}}.","Divyang Balnatya competition, final round: from {{DivyangFinalStart}}.","Professional Marathi drama competition: entries for the preliminary round are asked for each year in December. {{ProfessionalNote}}","The play must be shown on the day, at the place and at the time fixed by the Government."]},{"Key":"venue","Title":"Venue","Items":["The competition is held at the centres fixed by the Government: 19 centres in Maharashtra and 1 in Goa for the amateur drama competition. The centres and dates are announced by the Government. {{CentresNote}}","One-act play: the training round is held in the 36 districts, the preliminary round at six divisional centres and the final at one State-level centre. The training round plays are shown in a small hall (not a theatre)."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"Rounds and Qualification","Items":["Amateur Marathi drama: a preliminary round at the regional centres, then the final round. An institution must get at least 60 marks in the regional competition to be selected for the final.","At the final the institution must get at least 70, 65 or 60 marks to win the first, second or third prize.","Professional Marathi drama: a preliminary round, then 10 drama institutions are chosen for the final.","Hindi, Music and Sanskrit amateur drama: a final round at one centre each.","Balnatya: a preliminary round at 10 centres and a final round. A Balnatya institution must get at least 70, 65 or 60 marks at the final to win the first, second or third prize.","Divyang Balnatya: a final round from {{DivyangFinalStart}}.","One-act play: three rounds. Round 1, the training round (Taleem feri), in 36 districts. Round 2, the preliminary round, at 6 divisional centres. Round 3, the final, at State level.","One-act play, training round: there are three examiners at each centre. At least about 40 one-act plays are shown at a centre. At least five one-act plays are examined on one day. For every 10 plays shown at a centre, one is chosen for the preliminary round (10 to 1, 20 to 2, 30 to 3 and so on up to 100 to 10). A centre with fewer than 10 entries does not hold a training round, and its entries are joined to a nearby centre. The play may be an old or a new script. The artists must show the play within the time allowed.","One-act play, training round evaluation: the one-act play is shown without set, costume, background music, sound or lighting. It is judged mainly on the idea of the one-act play, the use of the artists' voices, movement and acting.","One-act play, preliminary round: there are three examiners at each centre. At least five entries must reach the centre. If fewer than five qualify, they are joined to a nearby centre. Up to 5 one-act plays are chosen from each centre for the final. The 5 plays chosen at each centre are ranked first, second, third, fourth and fifth.","One-act play, final round: 30 plays take part (5 from each of the 6 centres). Three examiners chosen by the Directorate choose the best 3 one-act plays for the first, second and third place.","One-act play: a college must get at least 60 marks to be selected from the training round to the preliminary round, and from the preliminary round to the final."]},{"Key":"points","Title":"Marks and Placings","Items":["An institution must get at least 60 marks in the regional competition to be chosen for the final round.","At the final the first, second and third prizes need at least 70, 65 and 60 marks.","An institution that gets an average of fewer than 40 marks for two years in a row is not allowed to take part in the competition for one year.","A new script (the first performance of the script) gets 5 extra marks in the total marks at evaluation, in the amateur drama competitions and in the one-act play training round.","The decision of the Government about prizes is final."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Awards and Prizes","Items":["Marathi, Hindi, Sanskrit and Music drama final awards: best play (first, second, third), direction, writing, lighting, set (nepathya), make-up, music or playback music direction, and acting prizes (cash and a silver medal each). The acting prizes are 2 men and 2 women in Hindi, Sanskrit and Music, and 10 men and 10 women in Marathi.","Music drama: in addition there are prizes for music accompaniment (organ and tabla) and for music singing.","Balnatya final awards: best play (first, second, third and an encouragement prize for a Divyang Balnatya), direction, writing, lighting, set, music direction, costume, make-up and acting (5 boys and 5 girls, each with cash and a silver medal).","Marathi preliminary round awards: best play (first, second, third), direction, lighting, set, make-up, and acting (one man and one woman, each cash and a silver medal).","Professional Marathi drama awards: best play (first, second, third), direction, writing, lighting, set, music, costume, make-up and acting (5 men and 5 women, each with cash and a silver medal).","Amounts in the 2017 Government Resolution (the latest amounts found; check with the Directorate for the current edition): Marathi preliminary {{PrizeMarathiPrelim2017}}.","Marathi final: {{PrizeMarathiFinal2017}}.","Hindi final: {{PrizeHindiFinal2017}}.","Sanskrit final: {{PrizeSanskritFinal2017}}.","Music final: {{PrizeMusicFinal2017}}.","Balnatya preliminary (5 centres): {{PrizeBalnatyaPrelim2017}}.","Balnatya final: {{PrizeBalnatyaFinal2017}}.","Professional Marathi final: {{PrizeProfessional2017}}.","One-act play preliminary round (6 centres), for each centre: {{PrizeEkankikaPrelim}}.","One-act play final: {{PrizeEkankikaFinal}}.","Drama production cost paid to institutions is under Money Paid to Competitors."]},{"Key":"conduct","Title":"Conduct","Items":["Institutions are bound by the Government rules for the competition. An institution that breaks the rules is declared ineligible.","An institution selected for the competition that does not perform at the centre and on the date fixed loses the deposit. Its artists cannot take part in the next year's competition. The period of ineligibility is one year.","One-act play: a college can give the Directorate a written note about a technical fault in its performance until the last one-act play is shown. After the results are announced, no complaint is accepted."]},{"Key":"judging","Title":"Judging","Items":["The play is judged by examiners (parikshak) appointed by the Directorate of Cultural Affairs. The rules print no marking scheme.","One-act play examiners: at least 35 years old, with experience of at least 10 plays in writing, direction, sound, lighting, set or acting. An examiner who has judged for three years in a row is not appointed for the next two years. An examiner is posted in a district other than the examiner's own district. Scholars and researchers of drama can also be examiners. At least one woman examiner at each centre, and an experienced examiner at each centre. One examiner is kept in reserve for every two centres. Examiners get an honorarium of {{ExaminerHonorarium}} for each one-act play examined."]},{"Key":"other","Title":"Other Matters","Items":["Professional Marathi drama competition (started 1986-87, the 37th edition at the time of the portal): to encourage professional drama institutions and the growth of Marathi drama. 25 to 30 professional institutions take part each year. The rules for this competition are not in the downloaded rule books.","The portal also gives rule books and the affidavit (undertaking) forms for the drama, Balnatya and one-act play competitions for download.","Colleges register on the portal for one-act plays. Examiners register on the portal as well."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"State Drama Competition rules (Marathi)","Url":"https://www.mahanatyaspardha.com/assets/pdf/%E0%A4%B0%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%9C%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AF%20%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%9F%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AF%20%E0%A4%B8%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AA%E0%A4%B0%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%A7%E0%A4%BE%20%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%AF%E0%A4%AE%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%B5%E0%A4%B2%E0%A5%80.pdf"},{"Title":"State Balnatya Competition rules (Marathi)","Url":"https://www.mahanatyaspardha.com/assets/pdf/%E0%A4%AE%E0%A4%B9%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%B0%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%B7%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%9F%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%B0%20%E0%A4%B0%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%9C%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AF%20%E0%A4%AC%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%B2%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%9F%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AF%20%E0%A4%B8%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AA%E0%A4%B0%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%A7%E0%A4%BE%20%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%AF%E0%A4%AE%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%B5%E0%A4%B2%E0%A5%80.pdf"},{"Title":"State One-Act Play (Ekankika) Competition rules (Marathi)","Url":"https://www.mahanatyaspardha.com/assets/pdf/%E0%A4%8F%E0%A4%95%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%82%E0%A4%95%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%95%E0%A4%BE%20%E0%A4%B8%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AA%E0%A4%B0%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%A7%E0%A4%BE%20%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%AF%E0%A4%AE%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%B5%E0%A4%B2%E0%A5%80.pdf"},{"Title":"Competition portal: online entries, rules, undertaking forms","Url":"https://www.mahanatyaspardha.com/"},{"Title":"Directorate of Cultural Affairs: competitions","Url":"https://www.mahasanskruti.org/pgeDca_Competition.aspx"}]}`),n=[],i=[{AgeGroup:"Balnatya",Source:"State Balnatya and Divyang Balnatya Competition Rules 2026-27, section 2(अः)",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["The Balnatya competition is for child artists under 15 years of age. The template's age range for this group differs."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- The Balnatya competition is for child artists under 15 years of age. The template's age range for this group differs.`},{AgeGroup:"Divyang Balnatya (Differently-Abled Children)",Source:"State Balnatya and Divyang Balnatya Competition Rules 2026-27, section 2(अः)",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Children with disabilities who are studying up to Class 10 can take part. The age condition for technicians is relaxed."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Children with disabilities who are studying up to Class 10 can take part. The age condition for technicians is relaxed.`},{AgeGroup:"Hausi (Amateur)",Source:"State Drama Competition Rules 2026-27 (Rajya Natya Spardha Niyamavali), sections 1 and Explanation",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["All artists must be amateurs and authorised members of the institution, and local residents of the district."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- All artists must be amateurs and authorised members of the institution, and local residents of the district.`},{AgeGroup:"Vyavsayik(Prof)",Source:"Competition portal mahanatyaspardha.com and Directorate of Cultural Affairs competitions page, professional drama competition",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["For professional drama institutions. The rule books downloaded do not give separate rules for this competition."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- For professional drama institutions. The rule books downloaded do not give separate rules for this competition.`}],o=[{Title:"Natya (Drama)",Source:"State Drama Competition Rules 2026-27 (Rajya Natya Spardha Niyamavali), sections 3 and 5",Sections:{About:["Used for the amateur Marathi, amateur Hindi and professional Marathi drama competitions."],TimeAndFormat:["Not less than 2 hours and not more than 3 hours, including intervals of 15 minutes each, with no more than two intervals."],Rules:["The institution must show the full play at the centre, on the day and at the time fixed.","An artist cannot act in more than one play of the same language in the competition."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Used for the amateur Marathi, amateur Hindi and professional Marathi drama competitions.

## Time and format
- Not less than 2 hours and not more than 3 hours, including intervals of 15 minutes each, with no more than two intervals.

## Rules
- The institution must show the full play at the centre, on the day and at the time fixed.
- An artist cannot act in more than one play of the same language in the competition.`},{Title:"Sangeet Natya (Musical Drama)",Source:"State Drama Competition Rules 2026-27 (Rajya Natya Spardha Niyamavali), section 6",Sections:{About:["All the rules of the Maharashtra State Natya Mahotsav apply to Music Marathi drama, with the changes below."],TimeAndFormat:["More than 3 hours and less than 4 hours."],Rules:["A play counts as a music play if its verses and songs are the main way of showing the story and feelings of the play. Folk drama (Lokanatya) is not counted as a music play.","Besides the artists who play a role, up to 12 persons (director, music director, prompter, instrumentalists and others) get the actual travel cost and stay cost at {{BoardingAllowance}} each a day. The entry must clearly say that the play is a music play, and the script must include verses.","Recorded sound and background music can be used for effect in a music play, but the verses and songs of the characters cannot be shown by playback singing."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:["The Marathi Music drama competition is counted as a separate competition from Marathi drama for the rule that an artist can act in only one play in a language."]},Markdown:`## About this competition
- All the rules of the Maharashtra State Natya Mahotsav apply to Music Marathi drama, with the changes below.

## Time and format
- More than 3 hours and less than 4 hours.

## Rules
- A play counts as a music play if its verses and songs are the main way of showing the story and feelings of the play. Folk drama (Lokanatya) is not counted as a music play.
- Besides the artists who play a role, up to 12 persons (director, music director, prompter, instrumentalists and others) get the actual travel cost and stay cost at {{BoardingAllowance}} each a day. The entry must clearly say that the play is a music play, and the script must include verses.
- Recorded sound and background music can be used for effect in a music play, but the verses and songs of the characters cannot be shown by playback singing.

## Notes for participants
- The Marathi Music drama competition is counted as a separate competition from Marathi drama for the rule that an artist can act in only one play in a language.`},{Title:"Sanskrit Natya (Sanskrit Drama)",Source:"State Drama Competition Rules 2026-27 (Rajya Natya Spardha Niyamavali), sections 1(5), 2(5) and 3(5)",Sections:{About:["Sanskrit schools (pathshala) and secondary schools can enter this competition. Primary and secondary schools are not admitted to the other competitions."],TimeAndFormat:["Not less than 45 minutes and not more than one hour. The play has no interval."],Rules:["No more than 10 artists can act in a Sanskrit play. Travel cost is paid for a maximum of 10 artists.","If the institution is a school or a pathshala, teachers and former students cannot take part in the play."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Sanskrit schools (pathshala) and secondary schools can enter this competition. Primary and secondary schools are not admitted to the other competitions.

## Time and format
- Not less than 45 minutes and not more than one hour. The play has no interval.

## Rules
- No more than 10 artists can act in a Sanskrit play. Travel cost is paid for a maximum of 10 artists.
- If the institution is a school or a pathshala, teachers and former students cannot take part in the play.`},{Title:"Ekankika (One-Act Play)",Source:"Maharashtra State Marathi One-Act Play (Ekankika) Competition Rules, annexes A to C (Government Resolution of 29 September 2025)",Sections:{About:["Marathi one-act play competition for college students, in three rounds: training round, preliminary round and final."],TimeAndFormat:["Not less than 30 minutes and not more than 45 minutes. Each college gets one hour for the show, set, light, music and sound. Each team gets at most 15 minutes to set the stage. A college that breaks this rule is not eligible for the competition."],Rules:["A one-act play has at least two characters.","A college that does not perform on the date, place and time given is not eligible for the next year's one-act competition, nor are the students who took part."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Marathi one-act play competition for college students, in three rounds: training round, preliminary round and final.

## Time and format
- Not less than 30 minutes and not more than 45 minutes. Each college gets one hour for the show, set, light, music and sound. Each team gets at most 15 minutes to set the stage. A college that breaks this rule is not eligible for the competition.

## Rules
- A one-act play has at least two characters.
- A college that does not perform on the date, place and time given is not eligible for the next year's one-act competition, nor are the students who took part.`},{Title:"Balnatya (Children's Drama)",Source:"State Balnatya and Divyang Balnatya Competition Rules 2026-27",Sections:{About:["State Balnatya (23rd) and Divyang Balnatya (8th) competitions, 2026-27."],TimeAndFormat:["Not less than 25 minutes and not more than 45 minutes."],Rules:["The Director of Cultural Affairs has the final right to accept or refuse a play proposed for the competition. The Director does not need to give reasons for refusing a play.","A prize-winning Balnatya cannot be shown again by the same institution or school, but other institutions or schools can show it.","Child artists in a Balnatya play cannot act in another Balnatya of the same State competition.","The institution must show the full play at the centre, on the day and at the time fixed. It must promise not to cancel it. If it cancels, the deposit goes to the Government treasury."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- State Balnatya (23rd) and Divyang Balnatya (8th) competitions, 2026-27.

## Time and format
- Not less than 25 minutes and not more than 45 minutes.

## Rules
- The Director of Cultural Affairs has the final right to accept or refuse a play proposed for the competition. The Director does not need to give reasons for refusing a play.
- A prize-winning Balnatya cannot be shown again by the same institution or school, but other institutions or schools can show it.
- Child artists in a Balnatya play cannot act in another Balnatya of the same State competition.
- The institution must show the full play at the centre, on the day and at the time fixed. It must promise not to cancel it. If it cancels, the deposit goes to the Government treasury.`}],r=[],s=JSON.parse(`[{"Key":"EntrySite","Kind":"link","Label":"Competition portal for online entries","Value":"https://mahanatyaspardha.com","Source":"Drama competition entries notice, 10 August 2026"},{"Key":"EntriesClose","Kind":"date","Label":"Last date to file entries (drama, Balnatya, Divyang Balnatya)","Value":"2026-08-31","Source":"Drama competition entries notice, 10 August 2026 and Balnatya competition entries notice, 10 August 2026 (the Directorate competitions page says 1 to 31 August each year)"},{"Key":"CompetitionStart","Kind":"date","Label":"Amateur Marathi, Hindi, Music and Sanskrit drama competitions start","Value":"2026-11-16","Source":"Drama competition entries notice, 10 August 2026 (the older Directorate competitions page says 15 November)"},{"Key":"BalnatyaPrelimStart","Kind":"date","Label":"23rd Balnatya competition, preliminary round starts","Value":"December 2026","Source":"Balnatya competition entries notice, 10 August 2026"},{"Key":"FinalRoundStart","Kind":"date","Label":"65th amateur Hindi, Music and Sanskrit competitions, final round starts","Value":"December 2026","Source":"Drama competition entries notice, 10 August 2026"},{"Key":"DivyangFinalStart","Kind":"date","Label":"Divyang Balnatya competition, final round starts","Value":"January 2027","Source":"Balnatya competition entries notice, 10 August 2026"},{"Key":"ProfessionalNote","Kind":"text","Label":"Professional Marathi drama competition","Value":"10 drama institutions are chosen for the final round.","Source":"Directorate of Cultural Affairs competitions page"},{"Key":"CentresNote","Kind":"text","Label":"Centres","Value":"The Directorate holds the competition at 19 centres in the State and 1 in Goa.","Source":"Directorate of Cultural Affairs competitions page"},{"Key":"DirectorateContact","Kind":"contact","Label":"Directorate of Cultural Affairs, Mumbai","Value":{"Name":"Directorate of Cultural Affairs, Maharashtra State, Mumbai","Role":"Old Secretariat, Extension Building, 1st floor, Mahatma Gandhi Marg, Mumbai 400032 (Mumbai, Konkan and Nashik division)","Phone":"022-22043550, 022-22842634, 022-22842670","WhatsApp":"","Email":"mahaculture@gmail.com"},"Source":"Competition portal mahanatyaspardha.com, Contact; Directorate of Cultural Affairs competitions page"},{"Key":"PuneOffice","Kind":"contact","Label":"Divisional office, Pune","Value":{"Name":"Assistant Director, Directorate of Cultural Affairs, Pune divisional office","Role":"Flat G-2/2, Shivkirti Government Officers Colony, near Ayyappa Temple, Ghorpadi Bazar, Pune 411001","Phone":"","WhatsApp":"","Email":""},"Source":"Competition portal mahanatyaspardha.com, Contact"},{"Key":"SambhajinagarOffice","Kind":"contact","Label":"Divisional office, Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar","Value":{"Name":"Assistant Director, Directorate of Cultural Affairs, Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar revenue division","Role":"Room 02, MTDC building, opposite Goldie Talkies, Station Road, Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar 431005","Phone":"8788893590","WhatsApp":"","Email":""},"Source":"Competition portal mahanatyaspardha.com, Contact"},{"Key":"NagpurOffice","Kind":"contact","Label":"Divisional office, Nagpur","Value":{"Name":"Assistant Director, Directorate of Cultural Affairs, Nagpur and Amravati division","Role":"Divisional office, c/o Curator, Central Museum, Temporary Exhibition Hall, ground floor, Civil Lines, Nagpur","Phone":"0712-2554211","WhatsApp":"","Email":""},"Source":"Competition portal mahanatyaspardha.com, Contact"},{"Key":"DepositAmateur","Kind":"fee","Label":"Deposit, amateur drama entry","Value":"Rs 3,000 (online, UPI)","Source":"State Drama Competition Rules 2026-27 (Rajya Natya Spardha Niyamavali), section 2(फ)"},{"Key":"DepositBalnatya","Kind":"fee","Label":"Deposit, Balnatya and Divyang Balnatya entry","Value":"Rs 1,000 (online, UPI)","Source":"State Balnatya and Divyang Balnatya Competition Rules 2026-27, section 2(ओ)"},{"Key":"DepositEkankika","Kind":"fee","Label":"Deposit, one-act play entry","Value":"Rs 2,000 (online)","Source":"Maharashtra State Marathi One-Act Play (Ekankika) Competition Rules, annexes A to C (Government Resolution of 29 September 2025), entry conditions 7"},{"Key":"ProductionCostPrelim","Kind":"fee","Label":"Production cost paid to an institution that performs the full play in the preliminary round","Value":"Rs 12,000","Source":"State Drama Competition Rules 2026-27 (Rajya Natya Spardha Niyamavali) 2(ग); State Balnatya and Divyang Balnatya Competition Rules 2026-27 2(औ)"},{"Key":"BoardingAllowance","Kind":"fee","Label":"Food and lodging allowance for each day","Value":"Rs 300","Source":"State Drama Competition Rules 2026-27 (Rajya Natya Spardha Niyamavali) 4; State Balnatya and Divyang Balnatya Competition Rules 2026-27 4(आ)"},{"Key":"EkankikaProductionCost","Kind":"fee","Label":"One-act play production cost for the preliminary round","Value":"Rs 10,000","Source":"Maharashtra State Marathi One-Act Play (Ekankika) Competition Rules, annexes A to C (Government Resolution of 29 September 2025), entry conditions 9"},{"Key":"EkankikaAllowance","Kind":"fee","Label":"One-act play daily allowance for food and lodging for each person","Value":"Rs 150","Source":"Maharashtra State Marathi One-Act Play (Ekankika) Competition Rules, annexes A to C (Government Resolution of 29 September 2025), students' travel and other costs 2"},{"Key":"WinnerShowGrant","Kind":"fee","Label":"Grant for the shows of winning Marathi and Music plays","Value":"Rs 10,000 for each show, Rs 50,000 for five shows","Source":"State Drama Competition Rules 2026-27 (Rajya Natya Spardha Niyamavali), section 7(ब)"},{"Key":"ExaminerHonorarium","Kind":"fee","Label":"One-act play examiner honorarium","Value":"Rs 500","Source":"Maharashtra State Marathi One-Act Play (Ekankika) Competition Rules, annexes A to C (Government Resolution of 29 September 2025), Annex B 9"},{"Key":"PrizeMarathiPrelim2017","Kind":"prize","Label":"Marathi amateur preliminary prizes (2017 Government Resolution, 21 centres)","Value":"best play 1st Rs 40,000, 2nd Rs 30,000, 3rd Rs 20,000; direction 1st Rs 20,000, 2nd Rs 10,000; lighting, set and make-up 1st Rs 10,000, 2nd Rs 6,000 each; acting (1 man and 1 woman) Rs 6,000 each with a silver medal","Source":"Government Resolution of 9 January 2017 (prize annexes 2 to 10), annex 2"},{"Key":"PrizeMarathiFinal2017","Kind":"prize","Label":"Marathi amateur final prizes (2017 Government Resolution)","Value":"best play 1st Rs 6,00,000, 2nd Rs 4,00,000, 3rd Rs 2,00,000; direction 1st Rs 1,00,000, 2nd Rs 80,000, 3rd Rs 60,000; drama writing 1st Rs 60,000, 2nd Rs 40,000, 3rd Rs 20,000; lighting, set, make-up 1st Rs 30,000, 2nd Rs 20,000, 3rd Rs 10,000 each; music, playback music and direction 1st Rs 30,000, 2nd Rs 20,000, 3rd Rs 10,000; acting (10 men and 10 women) Rs 20,000 each with a silver medal","Source":"Government Resolution of 9 January 2017 (prize annexes 2 to 10), annex 3"},{"Key":"PrizeHindiFinal2017","Kind":"prize","Label":"Hindi amateur final prizes (2017 Government Resolution)","Value":"best play 1st Rs 1,00,000, 2nd Rs 60,000, 3rd Rs 40,000; direction 1st Rs 40,000, 2nd Rs 30,000; writing 1st Rs 30,000, 2nd Rs 20,000; lighting, set, make-up 1st Rs 20,000, 2nd Rs 10,000 each; acting (2 men and 2 women) Rs 10,000 each with a silver medal","Source":"Government Resolution of 9 January 2017 (prize annexes 2 to 10), annex 6"},{"Key":"PrizeSanskritFinal2017","Kind":"prize","Label":"Sanskrit amateur final prizes (2017 Government Resolution)","Value":"best play 1st Rs 1,00,000, 2nd Rs 60,000, 3rd Rs 40,000; direction 1st Rs 40,000, 2nd Rs 30,000; writing 1st Rs 30,000, 2nd Rs 20,000; lighting, set, make-up 1st Rs 20,000, 2nd Rs 10,000 each; acting (2 men and 2 women) Rs 10,000 each with a silver medal","Source":"Government Resolution of 9 January 2017 (prize annexes 2 to 10), annex 7"},{"Key":"PrizeMusicFinal2017","Kind":"prize","Label":"Music amateur final prizes (2017 Government Resolution)","Value":"best play 1st Rs 1,50,000, 2nd Rs 1,00,000, 3rd Rs 50,000; direction 1st Rs 50,000, 2nd Rs 40,000; writing 1st Rs 40,000, 2nd Rs 30,000; music and playback music 1st Rs 30,000, 2nd Rs 20,000; set 1st Rs 30,000, 2nd Rs 20,000; organ accompaniment and tabla accompaniment 1st Rs 20,000, 2nd Rs 10,000 each; acting (2 men and 2 women) and music singing (2 men and 2 women) Rs 10,000 each with a silver medal","Source":"Government Resolution of 9 January 2017 (prize annexes 2 to 10), annex 8"},{"Key":"PrizeBalnatyaPrelim2017","Kind":"prize","Label":"Balnatya preliminary prizes (2017 Government Resolution, 5 centres)","Value":"best play 1st Rs 40,000, 2nd Rs 20,000, Divyang encouragement Rs 20,000; direction 1st Rs 20,000, 2nd Rs 10,000, Divyang encouragement Rs 10,000; lighting, set, make-up 1st Rs 10,000, 2nd Rs 6,000 each; acting (1 boy and 1 girl) Rs 6,000 each with a silver medal","Source":"Government Resolution of 9 January 2017 (prize annexes 2 to 10), annex 4"},{"Key":"PrizeBalnatyaFinal2017","Kind":"prize","Label":"Balnatya final prizes (2017 Government Resolution)","Value":"best play 1st Rs 1,00,000, 2nd Rs 60,000, 3rd Rs 40,000, Divyang encouragement Rs 20,000; direction 1st Rs 30,000, 2nd Rs 20,000, 3rd Rs 10,000, Divyang encouragement Rs 10,000; writing 1st Rs 30,000, 2nd Rs 20,000; lighting, set, music direction, costume, make-up 1st Rs 20,000, 2nd Rs 10,000 each; acting (5 boys and 5 girls) Rs 10,000 each with a silver medal","Source":"Government Resolution of 9 January 2017 (prize annexes 2 to 10), annex 5"},{"Key":"PrizeProfessional2017","Kind":"prize","Label":"Professional Marathi final prizes (2017 Government Resolution)","Value":"best play 1st Rs 7,50,000, 2nd Rs 4,50,000, 3rd Rs 3,00,000; direction 1st Rs 1,50,000, 2nd Rs 1,00,000, 3rd Rs 50,000; writing 1st Rs 1,00,000, 2nd Rs 60,000, 3rd Rs 40,000; lighting, set, music, costume, make-up 1st Rs 40,000, 2nd Rs 30,000, 3rd Rs 20,000 each; acting (5 men and 5 women) Rs 50,000 each with a silver medal","Source":"Government Resolution of 9 January 2017 (prize annexes 2 to 10), annex 9"},{"Key":"PrizeEkankikaPrelim","Kind":"prize","Label":"One-act play preliminary prizes for each centre (6 centres)","Value":"best one-act play 1st Rs 50,000, 2nd Rs 40,000, 3rd Rs 30,000, 4th Rs 20,000, 5th Rs 10,000; direction 1st Rs 20,000, 2nd Rs 10,000; writing 1st Rs 20,000, 2nd Rs 10,000; lighting, set (nepathya), make-up, music direction and costume 1st Rs 15,000, 2nd Rs 10,000 each; acting silver medal (1 man and 1 woman) Rs 10,000 each; total Rs 3,55,000 for each centre","Source":"Maharashtra State Marathi One-Act Play (Ekankika) Competition Rules, annexes A to C (Government Resolution of 29 September 2025), Statement 1"},{"Key":"PrizeEkankikaFinal","Kind":"prize","Label":"One-act play final prizes (1 centre)","Value":"best one-act play 1st Rs 1,00,000, 2nd Rs 75,000, 3rd Rs 50,000; direction 1st Rs 40,000, 2nd Rs 30,000, 3rd Rs 20,000; drama writing, lighting, set design, make-up, music direction and costume 1st Rs 40,000, 2nd Rs 20,000 each; acting silver medal 10 prizes (5 men and 5 women) Rs 10,000 each; total Rs 7,75,000","Source":"Maharashtra State Marathi One-Act Play (Ekankika) Competition Rules, annexes A to C (Government Resolution of 29 September 2025), Statement 2"},{"Key":"Announcements","Kind":"news","Label":"Notices printed in the 2026-27 rules","Value":[{"Date":"2026-08-10","Title":"Entries invited for the Maharashtra State Drama Competition, 2026-27","Text":"Entries for the amateur Marathi, Hindi, Music and Sanskrit drama competitions, which start on 16 November 2026, and for the Balnatya and Divyang Balnatya competitions are invited online until 31 August 2026.","Url":"https://mahanatyaspardha.com"},{"Date":"2026-08-10","Title":"23rd Balnatya and 65th Hindi, Music and Sanskrit rounds","Text":"The 23rd Balnatya preliminary round starts in December 2026 at 10 centres. The 65th Hindi, Music and Sanskrit final rounds are held at one centre each from December 2026. The Divyang Balnatya final starts in January 2027.","Url":""}],"Source":"Drama competition entries notice, 10 August 2026; Balnatya competition entries notice, 10 August 2026"}]`),l=[{Claim:"Rules, eligibility, entry procedure, deposits, costs, round structure and marks are from the 2026-27 State Drama, Balnatya and One-Act Play rule books on the official portal.",SourceType:"Primary - organiser's own rules",Source:"mahanatyaspardha.com rule books (Marathi), 2026-27",Url:"https://www.mahanatyaspardha.com/assets/pdf/%E0%A4%B0%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%9C%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AF%20%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%9F%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AF%20%E0%A4%B8%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AA%E0%A4%B0%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%A7%E0%A4%BE%20%E0%A4%A8%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%AF%E0%A4%AE%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%B5%E0%A4%B2%E0%A5%80.pdf"},{Claim:"Prize amounts for the drama and Balnatya finals are from the 2017 Government Resolution (annexes 2 to 10). One-act play prizes are from the 2025 Government Resolution.",SourceType:"Primary - Government resolution",Source:"Government Resolution RANASP 8616/PR.KR.91/SAKA.4 of 9 January 2017; Government Resolution SANKIRN 8224/PR.KR.316/SAKA.4 of 29 September 2025",Url:"https://www.mahanatyaspardha.com/Admin/Upload/GR/15.pdf"},{Claim:"Purpose, start year, centres and schedule are from the Directorate of Cultural Affairs competitions page.",SourceType:"Primary - organiser's own web page",Source:"Directorate of Cultural Affairs, competitions page",Url:"https://www.mahasanskruti.org/pgeDca_Competition.aspx"}],c={TemplateKey:t,Year:2026,Note:e,Page:a,ParticipantTypes:n,AgeGroups:i,Competitions:o,CompetitionAgeGroups:r,Variables:s,Evidence:l};export{i as AgeGroups,r as CompetitionAgeGroups,o as Competitions,l as Evidence,e as Note,a as Page,n as ParticipantTypes,t as TemplateKey,s as Variables,h as Year,c as default};
